Decision (mine): restore keeps every line outside the section byte-identical (split -1/join) and also keeps the current section's trailing blank lines (the gap before the next heading); only heading + text before them are restored, historic trailing blank lines are dropped. A plain -1 split would still lose the blank line whenever the restored version was the last section of its old page (its blank lines then lie outside the section). CRLF: the historic page gets Document::_read_page's page-level rule in _page_text_at (first line CRLF -> all CRLF to LF), so get_section_content_at_commit/restore (and get_section_commits) see Document's LF text; current_content is LF text by contract (k14). t/48: 1-8 pass, 12-14 pass with WB's uncommitted k14 Server/Document; 9-11 FAIL by design question: they stub get_section_content_at_commit with CRLF section text, which the real method never returns (normalisation is page-level, before the cut; a section-level rule would diverge from Document on mixed-ending pages). Verified real path: CRLF page committed via git -> section content LF, restore result has no \r. Also: real get_section_content_at_commit returns a trailing \n when the old section ended in a blank line (t/48 assumes none) - restore gives the same result for both (scratch: 3 variants x 6 cases). Patch of exactly k24's History.pm hunks: /tmp/mcp-wiki-k24/k24-history.patch (git apply --cached), since k23 edits the same file next.

